            <ArticleTitle>Nano-pulse stimulation induces potent immune responses, eradicating local breast cancer while reducing distant metastases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>629-640</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ijc.31071</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Nano-pulse stimulation (NPS) as a developing technology has been studied for minimally invasive, nonthermal local cancer elimination for more than a decade. Here we show that a single NPS treatment results in complete regression of the poorly immunogenic, metastatic 4T1-Luc mouse mammary carcinoma. Impressively, spontaneous distant organ metastases were largely prevented, even in those animals with incomplete tumor regression. All tumor-free mice were protected from secondary tumor cell challenge, demonstrating a vaccine-like effect. NPS treatment induced antitumor immunity, long-term memory T cells, destruction of tumor microenvironment and reversal of the massive increase of immune suppressor cells in the tumor microenvironment and blood. NPS-treated 4T1 cells exhibited release of damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs), including calreticulin, HMGB1 and ATP, and activated dendritic cells. Those findings suggest that NPS is a potent immunogenic cell death inducer that elicits antitumor immunity to prevent distant metastases in addition to local tumor eradication.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 UICC.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
